<p>The word doing the work in each provision is <em>sufficient</em> (or its mirror, <em>unable</em>). Sufficiency is not subsistence. The law does not ask whether the wife can avoid starvation on her salary; it asks whether her income sustains a life reasonably comparable to the one the marriage provided — housing, education, medical care, social existence — judged against the husband&rsquo;s own means and lifestyle.</p>

<h2>How the income gap is actually computed</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Disclose.</strong> Both parties file affidavits of assets, income and expenditure — the standardised disclosure that Family Courts now treat as the foundation of every maintenance determination.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Establish the standard.</strong> The matrimonial standard of living is reconstructed from housing, schooling, travel, staff and spending during cohabitation.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Measure both incomes.</strong> The wife&rsquo;s net actual income is set against the husband&rsquo;s — with courts determining income from lifestyle and records where disclosure is evasive, and imputing earning capacity to an able-bodied spouse who chooses idleness.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Bridge the shortfall.</strong> Maintenance is fixed to cover the gap between what the wife&rsquo;s income supports and what the marital standard requires, with children&rsquo;s needs computed separately.</div>
</div>

<h2>Recurring scenarios</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The modestly employed wife.</strong> A schoolteacher married to a senior executive is not disentitled; her salary reduces the quantum, it does not erase the claim.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The qualified but unemployed wife.</strong> Qualifications alone do not bar relief, but courts weigh genuine earning opportunity — a wife who left work for the marriage or children stands differently from one who declines available employment without reason.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The comparable earner.</strong> Where incomes are genuinely comparable, monthly support may be refused while litigation expenses are still allowed — the two components are independent.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The concealing husband.</strong> Understated income invites determination from conduct: EMIs serviced, cards used, businesses run through family members. Concealment routinely produces higher awards than candour would have.</div>
</div>

<h2>Advice for both sides</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Claimants:</strong> plead the marital standard with particulars and document your own income honestly — credibility on your side of the ledger is what makes the court sceptical of his.</p>
  <p><strong>Respondents:</strong> attack sufficiency, not employment. Proving that she earns is half an argument; the persuasive half is showing her income sustains the pleaded standard of living.</p>
  <p><strong>Both:</strong> the affidavit of assets is the case. Inaccuracies discovered later are treated as concealment, with consequences for quantum and credibility across the whole matrimonial litigation.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer states general principles of maintenance law and is not advice on any particular matter; quantum in any case turns on its own record of incomes, needs and standards.</div>
